{
  "_comment": "Every knob the generated fleet is built from. tools/gen_ship_data.py reads this file, data/weapons.json and data/tuning.json, and writes data/fleet.json. Retune the whole fleet by editing this file and running the tool again, never by editing the Python (CLAUDE.md section 5.4). data/ships.json is the hand authored catalog and is not touched by any of this.",

  "_tonnage": "Tonnage is per class and carries no faction modifier, on purpose. Three things read it: the skirmish command tonnage cap, the hull radius the sim collides, and the size ship_rig.gd draws a hull at. The meshes are normalised to one footprint per class (docs/18 section 5), so a faction scalar would make two hulls of the same footprint collide and draw at different sizes. A faction shows itself in shields, speed, boxes and mounts instead.",

  "_ladder": "The order tonnage has to climb in. It is the shipkit CLASSES table sorted by 'size', ties broken by 'foot', so the carrier, freighter and tender sit by how big the hull is rather than by how hard it fights. tools/gen_ship_data.py asserts this list still agrees with tools/shipkit.py.",
  "ladder": ["frigate", "destroyer", "light_cruiser", "heavy_cruiser", "tender",
             "freighter", "battlecruiser", "battleship", "carrier", "dreadnought"],

  "_headroom": "A hull is unfittable if its default weapons do not fit its budgets, and the fitting screen shows that red on first open. min_headroom_fraction is how much of each budget cap must still be free after the bare hull and its default weapons are counted, so there is room to refit upward rather than only sideways.",
  "min_headroom_fraction": 0.05,
  "min_headroom_absolute": 2,

  "_arcs": "The sector list a mount fires into: 12 sectors of 30 degrees, 0 dead ahead, clockwise (docs/02 section 4.1). 'facing' is which of the six shield facings the mount's hardware lives behind, numbered 1 to 6 from the bow clockwise and stored 0 based. The tool asserts the facing is one the sectors actually cover. Every arc here is symmetric about the centreline or has a mirror in the table, so a port battery and a starboard battery are the same gun.",
  "arcs": {
    "fore_spinal": { "sectors": [11, 0], "facing": 0 },
    "fore_wide":   { "sectors": [10, 11, 0, 1], "facing": 0 },
    "fore_stbd":   { "sectors": [0, 1, 2], "facing": 1 },
    "fore_port":   { "sectors": [9, 10, 11], "facing": 5 },
    "stbd_waist":  { "sectors": [2, 3, 4], "facing": 2 },
    "port_waist":  { "sectors": [7, 8, 9], "facing": 4 },
    "dorsal":      { "sectors": [9, 10, 11, 0, 1, 2], "facing": 0 },
    "rack_wide":   { "sectors": [8, 9, 10, 11, 0, 1, 2, 3], "facing": 0 },
    "waist_aft":   { "sectors": [3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8], "facing": 3 },
    "aft_wide":    { "sectors": [4, 5, 6, 7], "facing": 3 }
  },

  "_systems": "The internal boxes a hull can carry. family is what the damage model and the repair costs treat the box as (data/tuning.json repair): weapon, power, control, hull. Every code here has a committed mask in assets/icons, which the tool checks, because the ship systems display draws the icon from the code. The codes come from the hand authored hulls in data/ships.json, plus SENS, RPR and CARGO, which data/stations.json already names as stations. Nothing here is invented.",
  "systems": {
    "BRDG":  { "family": "control" },
    "SENS":  { "family": "control" },
    "TRAN":  { "family": "control" },
    "TRAC":  { "family": "control" },
    "MRNE":  { "family": "control" },
    "SHTL":  { "family": "control" },
    "DRN":   { "family": "control" },
    "PRB":   { "family": "control" },
    "LAB":   { "family": "control" },
    "RPR":   { "family": "control" },
    "WARP":  { "family": "power" },
    "IMP":   { "family": "power" },
    "AUXP":  { "family": "power" },
    "BTTY":  { "family": "power" },
    "CARGO": { "family": "hull" },
    "HULL":  { "family": "hull" },
    "ARMR":  { "family": "hull" }
  },

  "_placement": "Which shield facing each support system sits behind, and the order the boxes are listed in inside a facing. code_order runs power, then control, then hull, which is the order data/ships.json lists a facing in and the order the ship systems display reads. A code with two facings splits its boxes between them, which is what makes the warp drive two nacelles rather than one block. Weapons are listed first in their facing, in mount order, then these in this order.",
  "placement": {
    "BRDG": [0], "SENS": [0],
    "TRAN": [1],
    "WARP": [2, 4], "TRAC": [2],
    "IMP": [3], "AUXP": [3], "SHTL": [3], "DRN": [3],
    "BTTY": [4], "MRNE": [4], "PRB": [4],
    "LAB": [5], "RPR": [5],
    "CARGO": [2, 4]
  },
  "code_order": ["WARP", "IMP", "AUXP", "BTTY",
                 "BRDG", "SENS", "TRAN", "TRAC", "SHTL", "DRN", "MRNE", "PRB", "LAB", "RPR",
                 "CARGO"],

  "_weapon_boxes": "How much hardware a mount is, by what is fitted in it by default. The code the box wears is the weapon's own short from data/weapons.json, so the two cannot disagree. The omni entry is reserved and cannot be used yet: its short is OMNI and there is no assets/icons/omni.png, so a hull that defaulted to one would fail the generator's icon check. Add the icon in tools/gen_icons.py before pointing a mount plan at it. Players can still fit an omni by hand, because a special weapon ignores a mount's families.",
  "weapon_boxes": { "ph1": 4, "ph3": 2, "photon": 3, "disruptor": 4, "lance": 5, "drone": 2, "omni": 2 },

